"stimulus": ["Overconfidence bias is a common cognitive trait where an individual's subjective confidence in their judgments is reliably greater than the objective accuracy of those judgments. This is especially prevalent in tasks that individuals feel they have some control over, such as trading stocks or driving.", "This bias can lead to disastrous consequences. For example, business leaders may overestimate the success of a merger because they are overconfident in their own ability to integrate two distinct corporate cultures, leading to financial losses.", "Debiasing techniques, such as 'pre-mortems,' can help counter this effect. In a pre-mortem, individuals are asked to imagine that their plan has already failed and to generate reasons why this might have happened. This forces the mind to consider risks that were previously ignored due to over-optimism."],
