"stimulus": ["The 'male gaze' is a seminal concept in film theory, introduced by Laura Mulvey in her 1975 essay, 'Visual Pleasure and Narrative Cinema.' Mulvey argued that classical Hollywood cinema is structured around a masculine point of view. In this framework, the camera acts as an extension of the male protagonist’s perspective, positioning female characters as objects to be looked at—passive recipients of the male gaze rather than active agents of the narrative.", "Mulvey’s analysis combined psychoanalytic theory with feminism to explain how this structure reinforces patriarchal power dynamics within the audience. By identifying with the male hero, the male spectator is given the 'pleasure' of controlling the narrative and the female object on screen. This critique forced a radical re-examination of how cinematic pleasure is manufactured.", "While subsequent scholars have critiqued Mulvey for overlooking the diverse ways in which female and non-binary audiences engage with film, her work remains a cornerstone of film studies. It highlighted the systemic nature of visual representation and challenged the industry to consider the power inherent in the act of 'looking'."],
