"detail": {"question": "In what year was Mulvey's seminal essay 'Visual Pleasure and Narrative Cinema' published?", "correct": "1975", "distractors": ["1960", "1985", "1990"], "explanation": "The text explicitly dates the essay to 1975."},
"inference": {"question": "How does the 'male gaze' impact the male spectator according to the text?", "correct": "It provides a sense of power by allowing them to identify with the controlling male hero.", "distractors": ["It forces the male spectator to feel empathetic toward female characters.", "It distracts the male spectator from the narrative flow of the film.", "It encourages the male spectator to critically analyze his own behavior."], "explanation": "The text notes that by identifying with the male hero, the male spectator receives the 'pleasure' of controlling the narrative."},
"fourth": {"type": "summary", "question": "What was the lasting impact of Laura Mulvey’s theory on cinema studies?", "correct": "It initiated a critical re-examination of how cinematic visual representation reinforces power dynamics.", "distractors": ["It completely silenced the study of Hollywood cinema for several decades.", "It proved that all female directors exclusively follow the male gaze.", "It led to the immediate banning of Hollywood films by academic institutions."], "explanation": "The text explains that the work highlighted systemic visual representation and challenged the industry to consider power dynamics."}
