"stimulus": ["The evolution of animation has transformed from a labor-intensive manual craft into a technologically driven industry. Traditional hand-drawn animation, pioneered by studios like Disney, relied on the meticulous painting of thousands of individual cels to create the illusion of motion. Each second of film required roughly 24 frames, making it an incredibly time-consuming and expensive process that demanded highly skilled artists.", "The introduction of Computer-Generated Imagery (CGI) in the 1990s revolutionized the medium. By utilizing 3D modeling software, animators could manipulate virtual puppets within a three-dimensional space, allowing for more complex camera movements and lighting effects. This shift reduced the need for manual cel painting but introduced new challenges, such as the 'uncanny valley'—the unsettling feeling audiences experience when digital characters appear nearly, but not perfectly, human.", "Today, the lines between live-action and animation have become increasingly blurred. Photorealistic digital environments and motion-capture technology allow performers to infuse their digital avatars with human nuance, challenging the traditional definition of animation as a distinct, separate category."],
